Title: "Axiom Systems and Size"
Id: "4183ca80-b7a4-47be-9619-0f46dafc8553"


Text: "The elementary constructors established in previous sections—pairing,
       union, and power set—form only the scaffolding of modern set theory. To
       develop higher mathematics, analysis, and transfinite ordinal arithmetic,
       Zermelo--Fraenkel set theory incorporates powerful principles governing
       how predicates select elements from existing sets, how functional
       assignments generate new collections, and how ill-founded pathologies are
       excluded.

       Because first-order logic cannot quantify over arbitrary properties or
       predicates, principles such as Separation and Replacement cannot be
       expressed as single axioms. Instead, they are formulated as *axiom
       schemas*—infinite families of axioms, supplying one formal postulate for
       every well-formed formula $\varphi$ in the language $\mathcal{L}_{\in}$."
Id: "ddb95a61-dddb-4c5a-bb15-430be7091d5a"


SectionTitle: "Separation and Replacement"
Id: "b04301f4-2e38-4797-b6cd-626d4be8bcb7"


Axiom:
given:
. A is \set
. phi(x_) is (_ is \set) -> (_ is \\statement)
then:
. exists: B is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: x is \set
    then:
    . have: x "in" B
      iff:
      . x "in"? A
      . phi(x)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom Schema of Separation"
. description: "For every property $\varphi$, the elements of an existing set
                $A$ satisfying $\varphi$ form a set."
Id: "5077fbc7-a5c8-4d60-b1a7-a9d65097f003"


Axiom:
given:
. A is \set
. phi(x_, y_) is (_ is \set, _ is \set) -> (_ is \\statement)
where:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then:
  . existsUnique: y is \set
    suchThat: phi(x, y)
then:
. exists: B is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: y is \set
    then:
    . have: y "in" B
      iff:
      . exists: x "in" A
        suchThat: phi(x, y)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom Schema of Replacement"
. description: "The image of a set under a formula that assigns each input a
                unique output is again a set."
Id: "6e09ea04-f1dd-41c0-a0f5-4c962cd819ac"


Text: "The distinction between Separation and Replacement is of paramount
       foundational importance. The Axiom Schema of Separation (Zermelo's
       *Aussonderungsaxiom*) is conservative: it can only carve out subsets of an
       already existing set $A$. It can never leave the rank of $A$ or construct
       sets of larger size.

       The Axiom Schema of Replacement, introduced by Abraham Fraenkel and
       Thoralf Skolem in 1922, is an expansive generative principle: if a
       definable class operation associates each element of a set $A$ with a
       uniquely determined set $y$, then the entire collection of outputs
       $\{F(x) : x \in A\}$ forms a valid set. Replacement is indispensable for
       transfinite recursion and ordinal arithmetic, guaranteeing that sequences
       indexed by ordinals (such as $\aleph_\omega = \bigcup_{n < \omega}
       \aleph_n$) cohere into genuine sets."
Id: "1cbdad45-8bc2-4388-8a12-19b187499517"


SectionTitle: "Foundation and Choice"
Id: "960e9966-e880-421a-afa0-c8c753476a7c"


Axiom:
given: A is \(nonempty)::set
then:
. exists: a is \set
  suchThat:
  . a "in"? A
  . not:
    . exists: x is \set
      suchThat:
      . x "in"? A
      . x "in"? a
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Foundation"
. description: "Every nonempty set $A$ has a member disjoint from $A$."
Id: "cc3fc1c3-8ea9-45a6-874c-9215525952f4"


Axiom:
given:
. I, U is \set
. A(i__) is \function:on{I}:to{\power.set:of{U}}
where:
. forAll: i "in" I
  then:
  . exists: S is \set
    suchThat:
    . S = A(i)
    . S != \empty.set
then:
. exists: f(i__) is \function:on{I}:to{U}
  suchThat:
  . forAll: i "in" I
    then:
    . exists: S is \set
      suchThat:
      . S = A(i)
      . f(i) "in"? S
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Choice"
. description: "Every set-indexed family of nonempty subsets of an ambient set
                admits a choice function selecting one element from each member
                of the family. This ambient-set formulation is equivalent over
                ZF to the usual formulation for arbitrary set-indexed families."
Id: "3f1ec40e-207c-431e-918e-7a8f4f23fa46"


TextTheorem: "Over the base theory ZF, the Axiom of Choice (AC) is equivalent to
              a vast constellation of fundamental mathematical principles across
              algebra, topology, and analysis. Among the most prominent are:
              1. *Zermelo's Well-Ordering Theorem*: Every set can be endowed
                 with a well-ordering.
              2. *Zorn's Lemma*: Every non-empty partially ordered set in which
                 every chain possesses an upper bound contains at least one
                 maximal element.
              3. *Right Inverses*: Every surjective function admits a right
                 inverse (a section).
              4. *Linear Algebra*: Every vector space possesses a Hamel basis.
              5. *Topology*: Tychonoff's Theorem, asserting that any product of
                 compact topological spaces is compact.

              The equivalence of these formulations permits mathematicians to
              invoke Choice in whichever structural guise is best suited to the
              problem at hand."
Id: "694788bb-a939-406b-8a3f-62730d8d0399"


SectionTitle: "Cardinality and Cantor's Theorem"
Id: "ce8978c6-895a-4528-bc27-a1796c5fc751"


[A \.equinumerous.with./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. exists: f(x__) is \function:on{A}:to{B}
  suchThat: f is? \(bijective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ and $B?$ have the same cardinality"
. written: "|A?| = |B?|"
. description: "Two sets have the same cardinality when a bijection exists
                between them."
Id: "5ba55513-d2b3-458e-afd9-2fbd7507d47f"


[A \.cardinality.leq./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. exists: f(x__) is \function:on{A}:to{B}
  suchThat: f is? \(injective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}
Documented:
. called: "cardinality of $A?$ is at most cardinality of $B?$"
. written: "|A?| \leq |B?|"
. description: "$|A|\leq|B|$ means that an injection from $A$ to $B$ exists."
Id: "d3cc912e-a944-4a5f-8cc8-cca18c511746"


[\(finite)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies:
. exists: n is \natural
  suchThat: X \.equinumerous.with./ \natural.initial.segment:before{n}
Documented:
. adjective: "finite"
. description: "A set is finite when it is bijective with the natural numbers
                strictly below some natural number $n$."
Id: "88d1e87d-f649-4246-b417-7d3ca4c7f24c"


[\(countably.infinite)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies: X \.equinumerous.with./ \naturals
Documented:
. adjective: "countably infinite"
. description: "A countably infinite set is one admitting a bijection with the
                natural numbers."
Id: "7337910a-2ecc-46f7-95a7-9cc5812af19e"


[\(countable)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies:
. anyOf:
  . X is? \(finite)::set
  . X is? \(countably.infinite)::set
Documented:
. adjective: "countable"
. description: "A set is countable when it is finite or countably infinite."
Id: "9725d8a1-0c65-491e-901b-ee23b8e87888"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
where:
. A \.cardinality.leq./ B
. B \.cardinality.leq./ A
then: A \.equinumerous.with./ B
Documented:
. called: "Cantor--Schroeder--Bernstein Theorem"
. description: "Mutual injections between two sets imply the existence of a
                bijection."
Id: "5ad0453f-1203-4467-949b-4c8a8697b5b3"


Theorem:
given: A is \set
then:
. not: A \.equinumerous.with./ \power.set:of{A}
Documented:
. called: "Cantor's Theorem"
. description: "No set is equinumerous with its power set."
Proof: """Suppose for contradiction that there exists a bijection
          $f : A \to \mathcal{P}(A)$. In particular, $f$ must be surjective.
          Define the diagonal subset $D \subseteq A$ by Separation:
          $$D := \{x \in A : x \notin f(x)\}$$ Because $D \subseteq A$, $D$ is
          an element of the power set $\mathcal{P}(A)$. Since $f$ is surjective
          onto $\mathcal{P}(A)$, there must exist an element $d \in A$ such that
          $f(d) = D$. Now ask whether $d \in D$:
          - If $d \in D$, then by definition of $D$, $d \notin f(d) = D$, which
            is an immediate contradiction.
          - If $d \notin D$, then $d \notin f(d)$, which by definition of $D$
            implies $d \in D$, again a contradiction. Thus
            $d \in D \iff d \notin D$, which is a logical impossibility.
            Therefore no surjective function from $A$ to $\mathcal{P}(A)$ can
            exist, proving that $A$ is not equinumerous with $\mathcal{P}(A)$."""
Id: "83a30248-2172-45d8-934a-e7a54be66b12"


Text: "Cantor's diagonal argument is one of the grand turning points in
       intellectual history. It demonstrates that infinity is not a single
       monolith: there exists an unending, strictly increasing hierarchy of
       infinite cardinalities:
       $$|A| < |\mathcal{P}(A)| < |\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(A))| < \dots$$
       Moreover, the diagonal technique introduced here is the conceptual
       ancestor of the most profound limitative results of modern thought:
       Bertrand Russell's refutation of naive comprehension, Kurt Gödel's First
       Incompleteness Theorem, and Alan Turing's proof of the undecidability of
       the Halting Problem all derive their power from this same
       self-referential diagonal architecture."
Id: "8d163eb8-a148-43d1-80b3-38c54e51e278"
